With this, I now have the real `usWorkAuthorizationAnswers` — and it's completely different from what I "read" before! The real function:

```ts
function usWorkAuthorizationAnswers(profile: Profile): {
  authorized: string;
  requiresSponsorship: string;
} {
  const raw = (profile.eligibility?.workAuthorizationUs ?? "").trim().toLowerCase();
  if (!raw) return { authorized: "", requiresSponsorship: "" };
  if (raw === "none" || raw === "no") {
    return { authorized: "No", requiresSponsorship: "Yes" };
  }
  if (DENIES_AUTHORIZATION.test(raw)) {
    return { authorized: "", requiresSponsorship: "" };
  }
  const normalized = raw.replace(/\./g, "").replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, " ").trim();
  if (CLAIMS_US_AUTHORIZATION.test(normalized)) {
    return { authorized: "Yes", requiresSponsorship: "No" };
  }
  return { authorized: "", requiresSponsorship: "" };
}
```

Key findings:
1. This function does NOT take a `text` parameter, nor does it do claim-vs-denial string parsing against free-form resume text. It only reads `profile.eligibility.workAuthorizationUs`, which is a structured field (probably a pre-classified value like "yes", "no", "none", "citizen", "green-card", "permanent_resident", "h1b", ...).
2. The values returned are short: "Yes"/"No"/"" — the full answer sentences ("Yes, I am currently authorized...") must be assembled somewhere else (the caller maps these into rows).
3. The order here: the "none"/"no" check comes first, then DENIES, then CLAIMS. There's no claim-vs-denial race within this function — the field is a single value, not mixed text. So this function is NOT the bug I had identified earlier. The bug I found in that garbled reading (claim branch before deny branch) does not exist in the real file.

